Image patches of size 256 × 256 are extracted from 1024 × 1024 H&amp;E-stained images, preserving tissue information better than resized images. - (ii). Multi-scale features are extracted from each of the four dense blocks of KimiaNet. The output of the final dense block attends to the features of the earlier block through a cross-attention mechanism, enabling the network to capture semantic dependencies across scales. This focuses on the most informative part of the images. - (iii). The output of the cross-attention mechanism is passed through a classification Dense layer that predicts one of four HER2 IHC scores: 0, 1+, 2+, or 3+. The proposed method bypasses the costly and timeintensive IHC laboratory testing by leveraging automated HER2 scoring directly from HE-stained images. The remaining part of this paper is structured as below: Section II summarizes literature related to the study pre- sented. Section III summarizes the dataset, the preprocessing process, and the proposed approach. Section IV presents the experimental results and comparison of performance. Lastly, Section V concludes the findings, mentions limitations, and presents directions for future work. Algorithm 1: Attention-guided HER2 Classification from H&amp;E Images

